I’ll first inventory the pattern, metadata, reusable analysis scripts, and candidate-structure index. Then I’ll establish a chemically constrained phase list, health-screen competing CIF records, and only proceed to GSAS-II refinement after source challenges are anchored.

The chemistry points first to Ca–W–O products, especially CaWO₄, but the precursor list contains no reported amounts or ratio, so I will not impose a mass-balance ratio that is absent. I’m now extracting observed peak families and enumerating exact-composition records for CaWO₄, residual WO₃/CaO, and charge-balanced non-1:1 calcium tungstates.

The observed dominant series is already highly diagnostic of tetragonal scheelite CaWO₄ (for example the strong families near 18.43°, 28.54°, 31.27°, 34.01°, 46.93°, and 54.11°). A second coherent series at 22.94°, 23.42°, 24.23°, 26.44°, and 33.10° is consistent with residual monoclinic WO₃. I’m treating the available WO₃ polymorphs as competitors and will rank them against those independent families before any quantitative refinement. One nominal CaWO₄ record failed to parse; the other scheelite record passed the geometry screen.

A useful complication has emerged: the scheelite CaWO₄ peaks are substantially broader than the sharp WO₃-like peaks, so a single common profile would bias both source ranking and fractions. I’ll keep the instrument profile anchored to the sharp phase and test neutral/size/strain broadening separately for CaWO₄. Several small apparent peaks are not yet assigned; I’m holding CaO, alternate WO₃ structures, and non-1:1 Ca–W–O products on the watch list rather than forcing them into the model.

I’m now running the same neutral-first refinement recipe against four structurally distinct WO₃ records (orthorhombic, two monoclinic records, and triclinic), each from its own checkpoint and with the same CaWO₄ size challenge. This directly tests whether the apparent WO₃ assignment is polymorph-specific rather than merely formula-specific.

The source challenge now favors ambient monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c) over orthorhombic Pnma both before and after elaboration: approximately Rwp 13.42% → 12.56% versus 14.73% → 14.66%. A second monoclinic record gives essentially the same ranking while passing the geometry screen, so I will use that healthier record. The triclinic competitor is worse and its elaborated trial drives U negative, invalidating that comparison. I’m now examining the replay residual for genuine additional peak families before deciding whether a third crystalline phase is supportable.

CaO refines to only ~1–1.5 wt% and gives a negligible Rwp change; its size trial collapses at the lower bound and both low-index texture challenges are singular/divergent, so it fails the requested replay-stability gate. A coexisting fergusonite CaWO₄ model likewise collapses to effectively zero scale. Because distinct residual peaks remain near 19.29° and 22.01°, I’m now following the balance gate by enumerating non-1:1 charge-balanced Ca–W–O formulas and inspecting analogue parents.

The analogue search produced a strong, chemically exact fingerprint: Ca₃OsO₆ has predicted families which, after the refined zero shift, coincide with the unexplained peaks at about 18.74/19.29°, 22.01°, 30.71/31.38°, 32.18°, and 36.61/37.2°. I constructed an isovalent Ca₃WO₆ analogue by Os⁶⁺→W⁶⁺ substitution; the parent topology is P2₁/c, the ionic sizes are close, and the adapted CIF passes the geometry/occupancy screen. I’m now testing its scale before releasing its cell.

The three-phase inventory is now chemically coherent: scheelite CaWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and a Ca₃WO₆-type phase built by an isovalent W-for-Os substitution. I’m reconverging that common checkpoint now, then I’ll run the required neutral/broadening and source-replacement challenges before freezing the deliverables.

The neutral checkpoint converged at Rwp 10.48%; the earlier CaWO₄ size term improves it substantially, so it is real rather than a bookkeeping artifact. I’m now challenging all three phases from that exact neutral state with size-only, strain-only, and combined broadening; any bound or nonphysical result will be discarded and its saved values will not leak forward.

The first parallel launch only saved trial flags before the worker sessions were retired, so I discarded those incomplete comparisons. I’ve restarted the challenges serially from the untouched neutral checkpoint; that is slower, but it preserves a valid common baseline and complete refinements.

The broadening challenge is decisive. Only CaWO₄ supports a finite positive size term (~0.094 µm); WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆ size terms run to the neutral 10 µm ceiling, while every strain-containing trial becomes negative and is therefore nonphysical. I’m retaining only the CaWO₄ size term and replaying it to convergence before testing background, polarization, roughness, asymmetry, and texture.

The residual audit confirms repeated intensity—not position—errors: for example, the CaWO₄ 011 family at 18.43° is overpredicted while its 004 and higher-angle families are underpredicted. Peak positions are otherwise reproduced to roughly one step, and the left/right widths support the retained common profile plus CaWO₄ size term. This makes preferred orientation the next physically motivated test; the background-order comparison is finishing first so texture cannot absorb baseline curvature.

Polarization gives a large, replay-stable global improvement (Rwp 9.42% → 6.36%, refined factor 0.932) while keeping all phase scales positive. I’m treating that as provisional: roughness and texture are being compared from the same pre-polarization checkpoint, after which I’ll cross-seed the winning models to check that the angular trend is not a scale/background surrogate.

Texture is also real for CaWO₄: the [110] March axis is the clear single-axis winner (provisional Rwp 6.85%, ratio ~1.29), beating [001], [100], [101], and [112]. I’ll replay it and compare the minor phases’ low-index axes from the same checkpoint; then polarization, roughness, and texture will be cross-seeded in combination so only replay-stable terms survive.

The reciprocal nuisance check is exposing the expected coupling: texture plus either polarization or roughness fits very well with nearly identical fractions, but releasing both together drives a roughness coefficient negative and creates a background singularity. I will not retain that over-parameterized combination. The reciprocal cross-seed is running now; the final choice will favor the physical, lower-dimensional model if fractions and angular residuals remain equivalent.

The angular-model ranking is now stable: texture plus two-parameter roughness gives the most balanced low/middle/high residuals (regional Rwp 2.32/3.01/3.37%) and fractions differ from texture-plus-polarization by less than 0.04 wt%. The combined model is non-identifiable, so I’m retaining roughness only. Before using it, I’m re-screening and re-running the serious WO₃ and CaWO₄ source competitors in the full three-phase inventory.

The scratch full-inventory launch is invalid: adding all three phases before the common two-phase profile let the dominant CaWO₄ size term hit its neutral ceiling and trapped the scales in a poor basin. Per the reversible-loop rule, I stopped that comparison and am restarting each source from its saved, independently converged two-phase checkpoint before adding Ca₃WO₆; none of the failed values will be reused.

The full-inventory source ranking is unambiguous: monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ gives Rwp 9.42% before intensity corrections and 2.924% after them; Pnma remains at 11.85% and 8.13%. Tetragonal WO₃ requires a negative U term and fits poorly, while fergusonite CaWO₄ drives X negative and size to its bound. I’m now doing the final watch-list replay for residual CaO and same-formula CaWO₄ coexistence.

CaO is a genuine borderline case rather than a collapsed scale: it replays at 0.27–0.29 wt% (positive by roughly 4–7σ) and improves two effectively independent families, 111 near 31.91° and 220 near 53.50°. Its size term is unsupported and stays at the 10 µm neutral ceiling, so I’m pruning that term while retaining CaO provisionally; the same-formula CaWO₄ watch model will be challenged once more against this four-phase checkpoint.

The four-phase model is now stable, with CaO at roughly 0.29 wt% and supported by multiple reflections. I’m doing the final watch-list and broadening re-challenges now, then I’ll replay the accepted model and export all deliverables from that exact project.

The CaO broadening challenges failed cleanly: strain refined negative, while size+strain collapsed the CaO scale and cell and became singular. That supports the neutral CaO profile and requires restoring the saved four-phase checkpoint. The same-formula polymorph check only failed because I used the database identifier as a filename; I’m resolving the actual record path and rerunning it.

The fergusonite CaWO₄ competitor collapsed to the scale floor before cell refinement; freeing its cell then made the metric invalid. It therefore fails both the positive-scale and replay-stability gates, so only scheelite CaWO₄ is retained. I’m now checking that the chosen eight-term smooth background still outranks adjacent orders in the accepted four-phase model.

The background loop found a clear boundary: 14 coefficients materially improve χ² over 12, while 16 adds only Δχ²≈8 for two parameters and is unsupported. Phase fractions move by at most about 0.04 wt% across the accepted orders. I’m adopting the 14-term smooth background and replaying every inactive phase-width option from that checkpoint to ensure the inventory does not depend on the background choice.

The post-background source replay is decisive: the retained monoclinic WO₃ model gives Rwp ≈2.90%, while the orthorhombic competitor remains at ≈8.11%; the fergusonite CaWO₄ scale again hits the floor. Width trials leave WO₃ strain statistically weak (100±73 microstrain) and all other added phase-width terms neutral, unphysical, or bound. I’m completing the corresponding texture/roughness/polarization checks from the same checkpoint.

The accepted terms survive: removing roughness or the CaWO₄ [110] texture substantially degrades/rebalances the fit, while anisotropic CaWO₄ size gives only a marginal extra gain. The polarization-plus-roughness trial is behaving as the earlier one did—very high scale/polarization/roughness correlations and slow, nonconvergent drift—so it will be rejected unless the replay reaches a stable physical endpoint.

A symmetry-compatible CaWO₄ size ellipsoid along the tetragonal c axis is supported: 0.0934(8) µm in the basal direction versus 0.0869(8) µm axially, reducing χ² by about 78 with one added parameter. The earlier [110] anisotropy was less effective and not symmetry-preferred. I’m accepting the [001] model and replaying the closest background, strain, weak-phase, and source competitors once more from it.

The final loop is stable. Fourteen background terms remain preferred (12 is worse; 16 gains too little for two parameters), CaWO₄/WO₃ strain terms are unsupported, CaO size stays neutral, the Pnma WO₃ source remains far worse (Rwp 8.10%), and fergusonite again collapses to zero. I’m now replaying the exact accepted model over the full range into `final.gpx`, then exporting CIFs and the profile from that project only.

`final.gpx` replayed cleanly (maximum shift/σ 0.0018). The exported crystalline fractions are 78.538% CaWO₄, 11.656% WO₃, 9.522% Ca₃WO₆, and 0.285% CaO, normalized to the modeled crystalline phases. I’m health-screening the exported CIFs now and then will run the cross-file consistency audit.

Completed and validated against the final GSAS-II replay.

